<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lahore</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lahore is a major city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, with a long history as a centre of political power. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> from 1799, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> took it, and the seat of the Lahore Darbar. Earlier, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> passed through Lahore on his invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahore.jpg" alt="Lahore" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rohaan Bhatti · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lahore_Fort_view_from_Baradari.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Indian campaign was perhaps his most spectacular achievement: forcing the Khyber Pass in late 1738, he took Peshawar, crossed the Indus, occupied <b>Lahore</b> and, after Karnal, entered Delhi in March 1739.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Qutb ud-Din Aibak, a Turkic slave-general who had governed the Ghurid conquests in northern India from Delhi since the victory at Tarain in 1192, assumed independent power at <b>Lahore</b>, founding the Mamluk or Slave Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmad Shah's eight invasions brought Kashmir and most of the Punjab under Afghan rule, with <b>Lahore</b> governed by Afghans, and the sack of Delhi in 1757 yielded the booty he coveted; the Mughal emperor was left in nominal control of his city as long as he acknowledged Afghan…</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1040 <b>Lahore</b> and the Punjab became increasingly central to the economy and to the life of the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He captured Multan in 1175 but was defeated by the Chaulukyas of Gujarat at Kasahrada in 1178, and he ended Ghaznavid rule in the Punjab by taking <b>Lahore</b> in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The great majority of the population were peasants producing staples such as wheat, rice and cotton, while cities such as Agra, Delhi and <b>Lahore</b> functioned as hubs of commerce and administration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
